{"id":"neet-2026r-q137","pn":137,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Bt toxin","q":"The inactive form of Bt toxin is converted to the active form in the insect gut _______","o":["due to acidic pH","by proteases","by nucleases","due to alkaline pH"],"c":3,"s":"Bt toxin protein exists as inactive protoxin.\nWhen an insect eats it, the alkaline pH of the gut solubilises the crystals.\nThis converts the protoxin into the active toxin."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q116","pn":116,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Human insulin","q":"Which of the following genetically engineered organisms was used by Eli Lilly to prepare human insulin?","o":["Bacterium","Yeast","Virus","Phage"],"c":0,"s":"The correct answer is bacterium.\nIn 1983, Eli Lilly, an American company, prepared two DNA sequences matching the 'A' and 'B' chains of human insulin.\nThey introduced them into plasmids of E.coli (a gram negative bacterium) to produce the insulin chains."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q131","pn":131,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Bt toxin","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Bt toxins are insect group specific and coded by a gene cry IAc.\nStatement II : Bt toxin exists as inactive protoxin in B. thuringiensis. However, after ingestion by the insect the inactive protoxin gets converted into active form due to acidis pH of the insect gut.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"c":0,"s":"Bt toxin genes were isolated from Bacillus thuringiensis and put into crops such as cotton.\nThe gene chosen depends on the crop and the target pest, because most Bt toxins are insect-group specific.\nThe toxin is coded by a gene called cry. For example, cry IAc and cry IIAb control cotton bollworms, and cry IAb controls corn borer.\nSo Statement I is true.\n(Statement II is false: the protoxin is activated by the alkaline pH of the insect gut, not an acidic one.)\nNote: The printed solution does not discuss Statement II; the remark about alkaline pH is from standard NCERT text."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q123","pn":123,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Genome sequencing: ESTs","q":"Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) refers to","o":["Certain important expressed genes.","All genes that are expressed as RNA.","All genes that are expressed as proteins.","All genes whether expressed or unexpressed."],"c":1,"s":"All the genes that are expressed as RNA are called Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs)."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q140","pn":140,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"RNA interference","q":"Transposons can be used during which one of the following ?","o":["Gene Silencing","Autoradiography","Gene sequencing","Polymerase Chain Reaction"],"c":0,"s":"The complementary RNA used in RNAi can come from mobile genetic elements (transposons) that replicate through an RNA intermediate.\nAutoradiography usually follows hybridisation, so it needs no transposons.\nPCR only copies DNA and needs no transposons.\nGene sequencing does not need transposons either."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q149","pn":149,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Gene therapy and gene gun","q":"Match List-I with List-II :\nList-I: (a) Gene gun (b) Gene therapy (c) Gene cloning (d) Genome\nList-II: (i) Replacement of a faulty gene by a normal healthy gene (ii) Used for transfer of Gene (iii) Total DNA in the cells of an organism (iv) To obtain identical copies of a particular DNA molecule\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"c":1,"s":"A gene gun transfers DNA into plant cells.\nGene therapy replaces a faulty gene with a healthy one.\nGene cloning makes identical copies of a DNA piece.\nThe genome is the total DNA of an organism's cell."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q166","pn":166,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Gene therapy ADA deficiency","q":"In gene therapy of Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency, the patient requires periodic infusion of genetically engineered lymphocytes because :","o":["Gene isolated from marrow cells producing ADA is introduced into cells at embryonic stages","Lymphocytes from patient's blood are grown in culture, outside the body.","Genetically engineered lymphocytes are not immortal cells.","Retroviral vector is introduced into these lymphocytes."],"c":2,"s":"The engineered lymphocytes are not immortal and die after some time, so infusions must be repeated.\nGrowing the patient's lymphocytes in culture is true but is not the reason.\nIf the gene were put into early embryonic cells, it could give a permanent cure."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q116","pn":116,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Gene therapy","q":"When gene targetting involving gene amplification is attempted in an individual's tissue to treat disease, it is known as :","o":["Safety testing","Biopiracy","Gene therapy","Molecular diagnosis"],"c":2,"s":"Gene therapy corrects a gene defect by delivering a working gene into the individual's cells.\nBiopiracy is unauthorised use of bio-resources, and molecular diagnosis is only identifying a disease."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q150","pn":150,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Molecular diagnosis, probes","q":"Now a days it is possible to detect the mutated gene causing cancer by allowing radioactive probe to hybridise its complimentary DNA in a clone of cells, followed by its detection using autoradiography because :","o":["mutated gene does not appear on photographic film as the probe has complementarity with it","mutated gene partially appears on a photographic film","mutated gene completely and clearly appears on a photographic film","mutated gene does not appear on a photographic film as the probe has no complementarity with it"],"c":3,"s":"The radioactive probe is made to match the normal gene.\nIt hybridises (pairs) only with the normal complementary DNA, which then shows up on the film.\nThe mutated gene has a changed sequence, so the probe cannot pair with it and it does not appear on the film."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q029","pn":29,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Insulin","q":"Which of the following statements is not correct ?","o":["The proinsulin has an extra peptide called C-peptide","The functional insulin has A and B chains linked together by hydrogen bonds","Genetically engineered insulin is produced in E-coli","In man insulin is synthesised as a proinsulin"],"c":1,"s":"Mature insulin has chains A and B joined by disulphide bonds, not hydrogen bonds.\nSo statement (2) is not correct."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q071","pn":71,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Applications of biotechnology","q":"Match the following columns and select the correct option.\n(a) Bt cotton  (i) Gene therapy\n(b) Adenosine deaminase deficiency  (ii) Cellular defence\n(c) RNAi  (iii) Detection of HIV infection\n(d) PCR  (iv) Bacillus thuringiensis","o":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"c":3,"s":"Bt cotton - Bacillus thuringiensis.\nADA deficiency - gene therapy.\nRNAi - cellular defence.\nPCR - detection of HIV infection."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q074","pn":74,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Bt crops","q":"Bt cotton variety that was developed by the introduction of toxin gene of Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) is resistant to :","o":["Fungal diseases","plant nematodes","Insect predators","Insect pests"],"c":3,"s":"Bt toxin kills insect pests such as bollworms, so Bt cotton resists insect pests."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q036","pn":36,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Biofortified crops","q":"Which of the following is true for Golden rice?","o":["It is drought tolerant, developed using Agrobacterium vector.","It has yellow grains, because of a gene introduced from a primitive variety of rice.","It is Vitamin A enriched, with a gene from daffodil.","It is pest resistant, with a gene from Bacillus thuringiensis."],"c":2,"s":"Golden rice is rich in vitamin A.\nIts gene for beta-carotene comes from daffodil."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q059","pn":59,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Biotechnology and its Applications","st":"Bt toxin","q":"What triggers activation of protoxin to active Bt toxin of Bacillus thuringiensis in boll worm ?","o":["Alkaline pH of gut","Acidic pH of stomach","Body temperature","Moist surface of midgut"],"c":0,"s":"Bt protoxin is an inactive crystal.\nThe alkaline pH of the insect gut dissolves it and activates it."}
